ning for time, place, and person.</div><div><br></div><div>ST:</div><div>&nbsp;- Direct swallowing therapy: successfully trialed IDDSI-0 liquid via small medicine cups (5-10mL per sip, total 50mL) with zero choking, normal oral/pharyngeal transit times (1-2s), and zero wet voice.</div><div>&nbsp;- Progressed rapidly following self-removal of NG tube on 06/26, fully tolerating solid and semi-liquid oral intake. Swallowing training shifted to safety maintenance.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>[Differential Diagnoses, Evaluations, and Missing Information]</div><div><br></div><div>Differential Diagnoses &amp; Clinical Reasoning:</div><div>1. Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH): The classic triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and cognitive decline was present for 5 months prior to tumor surgery. 06/20 Brain CT notes "R/O mild communicating hydrocephalus". A left frontal parasagittal tumor explains some frontal symptoms, but hydrocephalus might be a co-existing or post-surgical compounding factor.</div><div>2. Post-operative Delirium vs. Tumor-Associated Cognitive Impairment: Fluctuating orientation, poor MMSE (9/30), 4AT score of 5, and occasional PRN use of Haloperidol/Anxicam indicate active or resolving delirium superimposed on baseline frontostriatal impairment.</div><div>3. Extrapyramidal Syndrome / Parkinsonism: Patient has a history of tremors, slow response, left arm cogwheel rigidity, and tongue fasciculation. While a frontal lesion can cause pseudo-parkinsonian gait, true Parkinson's disease (G20 listed in OPD) or drug-induced parkinsonism (from post-op Haloperidol) must be differentiated.</div><div>4. Overactive Bladder / Neurogenic Bladder: Long-term multi-drug urological regimens (Propiverine, Solifenacin, Imipramine) are documented. Note: Raw chart mentions "History of Prostate enlargement post TURP (2017)", but demographics state "77-year-old female". This is a critical documentation contradiction.&nbsp;</div><div><br></div><div>Recommended Evaluations &amp; Managements:</div><div>- Neurosurgical follow-up on the communicating hydrocephalus (consider a lumbar tap test if gait and cognition worsen, or Evans index surveillance on repeat imaging).</div><div>- Strict medication review (Deprescribing check): The patient is simultaneously taking Solifenacin, Propiverine, and Imipramine. This high anticholinergic burden can severely worsen post-operative delirium, cognitive scores (MMSE 9/30), and central processing.</div><div>- Delirium prevention protocols, sleep-wake cycle re-establishment, and tapering off Haloperidol/Anxicam as soon as behavior stabilizes.</div><div>- Dedicated Pusher Syndrome correction protocols in PT (using visual vertical cues, mirror feedback, and forcing weight shifts to the left/stronger side).</div><div><br></div><div>Missing Information / Contradictions in Documentation:</div><div>- Gender/Anatomical Contradiction: The raw summary explicitly begins with "77 year-old female patient", yet lists a "History of Prostate enlargement post Transurethral resection of prostate (TURP) on 2017/05/23" and an Abdomen CT showing "Prostate enlargement". This conflict requires immediate verification of the patient's biological sex and past surgical history.</div><div>- Missing Timepoint PE: There is an absolute lack of documented Physical / Neurological Examination at the ER/Admission timepoint, and immediately post-surgery/ICU stay before rehabilitation started.&nbsp;</div><div>- Ileus Follow-up: 06/15 Abdomen CT showed obstructive ileus at the distal small bowel level. There is no documentation regarding how this ileus was managed (conservative, decompression, or surgical) or its current status upon transfer to PMR.</div><div>- Laboratory Data: No specific post-operative lab values (hemogram, electrolytes, renal function, or HbA1c/lipid workup for the documented atherosclerosis) were included in the raw text.</div></div><div><br></div><div>[Present illness]</div><div><div>This is a 77-year-old man with a medical history of&nbsp;</div><div># Left frontal parasagittal region meningioma, nearly 3.3x3.1 cm, status post diagnostic cerebral angiography on 2026/06/17 and bicoronal craniotomy with tumor excision (Simpson grade II) under microscope assistance with subdural ICP monitoring line placement on 2026/06/18, with post-operative cognitive impairment, pusher syndrome, and deconditioned status</div><div><span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;"># Hypertension</span></div><div># Ileus, status post abdomen CT on 2026/06/15 showing dilatation and fluid-filled proximal small bowels suggestive of distal small bowel obstructive ileus, mild mesenteric edema at RLQ abdomen, non-specific minimal fluid in abdominal/pelvic cavity, and small loculated fluid at pelvic wall</div><div><span style="font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"># Multiple small gallbladder stones with slight gallbladder distension</span></div><div># History of varicose veins of left lower extremities, status post left endovenous radiofrequency ablation on 2018/09/12</div><div># History of prostate enlargement, status post transurethral resection of prostate (TURP) on 2017/05/23</div><div><br></div><div>The patient experienced a gradual onset of gait disturbance, tremors, slow response, urinary incontinence, and recent dizziness for about 4 months before admission. He visited the Neurology outpatient department on April 13, 2026. A brain CT was arranged and revealed a left frontal lesion, and a subsequent enhanced brain MRI on June 3, 2026, confirmed a nearly 3.3x3.1 cm left frontal parasagittal meningioma with perifocal edema and leucoaraiosis. He was then admitted to the neurosurgery ward on June 13, 2026, at that time,&nbsp;<span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;">&nbsp;</span><span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;">and muscle power was documented as upper extremities 4 and lower extremities 3</span>. Propiverine 15mg QD and Solifenacin 5mg QD were prescribed for an overactive bladder. Due to ileus symptoms, an abdominal CT was performed on June 15. On June 18, 2026, he underwent bicoronal craniotomy and tumor excision with subdural ICP monitoring line placement. Imipramine 25mg BID was added post-operatively for overactive bladder. A follow-up brain CT on June 20 showed status post excision without demonstrable residual tumor, mild postoperative encephalomalacia/edema, minimal blood clot at the left frontal lobe, mild epidural fluid and minimal blood at the bilateral midline frontal region, senile brain change, and mild communicating hydrocephalus. Post-operatively, he developed cognitive fluctuations and delirium. Anxicam and Haloperidol were used occasionally.&nbsp;<span style="background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255); font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;, &quot;Noto Color Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;">Briviact was used for seizure prophylaxis.&nbsp;</span>He self-removed his NG tube on June 26 and tolerated a semi-liquid diet. He was transferred to our rehabilitation ward on July 2, 2026.</div><div><br></div><div>During the neurosurgery ward hospitalization, physiatrist was consulted and he received physical, occupational, and speech therapy. Physical therapy focused on correcting his right-leaning pusher syndrome, optimizing sitting and standing balance, and trunk control, which allowed him to achieve 8 meters of level walking with a walker requiring moderate support. Occupational therapy addressed ADL training—including rolling over, transitioning from side-lying to sitting, bed mobility, and bridging—along with upper and lower extremity dressing support for shoes and socks, and cognitive perceptual orientation training for time, place, and person. Speech therapy provided direct swallowing therapy, where he successfully trialed IDDSI-0 liquid via small medicine cups (5–10 mL per sip, 50 mL total) with normal oral and pharyngeal transit times of 1–2 seconds, no choking, and no wet voice. Following his self-removal of the NG tube on June 26, he progressed to fully tolerate semi-liquid oral intake, shifting speech therapy focus to safety maintenance.</div><div><br></div><div><div style="--tw-border-spacing-y: 0; --tw-translate-x: 0; --tw-translate-y: 0; --tw-rotate: 0; --tw-skew-x: 0; --tw-skew-y: 0; --tw-scale-x: 1; --tw-scale-y: 1; --tw-pan-x: ; --tw-pan-y: ; --tw-pinch-zoom: ; --tw-scroll-snap-strictness: proximity; --tw-gradient-from-position: ; --tw-gradient-via-position: ; --tw-gradient-to-position: ; --tw-ordinal: ; --tw-slashed-zero: ; --tw-numeric-figure: ; --tw-numeric-spacing: ; --tw-numeric-fraction: ; --tw-ring-inset: ; --tw-ring-offset-width: 0px; --tw-ring-offset-color: #fff; --tw-ring-color: rgb(59 130 246 / 0.5); --tw-ring-offset-shadow: 0 0 #0000; --tw-ring-shadow: 0 0 #0000; --tw-shadow: 0 0 #0000; --tw-shadow-colored: 0 0 #0000; --tw-blur: ; --tw-brightness: ; --tw-contrast: ; --tw-grayscale: ; --tw-hue-rotate: ; --tw-invert: ; --tw-saturate: ; --tw-sepia: ; --tw-drop-shadow: ; --tw-backdrop-blur: ; --tw-backdrop-brightness: ; --tw-backdrop-contrast: ; --tw-backdrop-grayscale: ; --tw-backdrop-hue-rotate: ; --tw-backdrop-invert: ; --tw-backdrop-opacity: ; --tw-backdrop-saturate: ; --tw-backdrop-sepia: ; --tw-contain-size: ; --tw-contain-layout: ; --tw-contain-paint: ; --tw-contain-style: ;">Upon admission to our rehabilitation ward on July 2, 2026, physical examination showed the patient was in fair spirit with stable vital signs under ambient air. Consciousness was E4M6V5, but he was disoriented, following simple 1-step commands but 2-step commands inconsistently. Cognitive and language evaluations revealed delirium and cognitive impairment, with a 4AT score of 5/12, an MMSE score of 9/30, and a LAST-A score of 10/15. Hemineglect testing showed a negative preferential gaze but poor cognition with mixed markings on the star cancellation test (5/54) and bisection test. Muscle strength (MMT) revealed asymmetric mild weakness: right upper and lower extremities were 3+/3+; left upper and lower extremities were 4/4. Neurological signs included positive Gegenhalten, tongue fasciculation, intention tremor, mild cogwheel rigidity in the left arm, disinhibition, and pseudobulbar affect. Suspected pusher syndrome was noted with leaning to the right. Regarding sphincter function, he was partially continent for defecation, occasionally using a commode or toilet, and incontinent for urination, utilizing a diaper. Regarding swallowing performance, his tongue movement was full, RSST was 5 times, and cough ability was functional. A 3 mL swallow trial showed absent oral stasis, drooling, premature spillage, choking, or wet voice, with full larynx elevation taking less than 1 second. He recovered quickly to total oral intake on a normal diet without choking, scored at FOIS 6. Regarding functional status, he was totally dependent in ADLs, with a Barthel Index of 25/100 (Feeding 5, Transfer 0, Grooming 5, Toilet 5, Bathing 0, Walking 0, Stairs 0, Dressing 5, Bowel 5, Bladder 0) and a modified Rankin Scale (mRS) of 4. His sitting balance was fair statically and poor dynamically, characterized by poor trunk control and a rightward deviation. His standing balance was poor statically, and dynamic standing was not applicable. At best, he could perform rolling over and sitting up with minimal contact guard, stand up with minimal assistance, transfer with moderate assistance, and ambulate with a walker and moderate assistance while leaning to the right due to pusher syndrome, graded at FAC 1. The TUG test was not applicable.</div></div><div><br></div><div>For these neurological deficits, he was admitted to our rehabilitation ward on July 2, 2026, for intensive rehabilitation.</div><div><br></div><div>[Premorbid]</div><div>Living environment: Lived with his wife and son in an apartment at Xiaojia East Road (discrepancy between a 2-story or 6-story apartment with an elevator)</div><div>Occupation: Retired (Background: University, Business Administration)</div><div>Education: University (Business Administration)</div><div>Caregiver: Family</div><div>Ambulation: Community ambulator without assistive devices or assistance (though gait disturbance for 5 months and legs losing strength with rare outdoor excursions were noted prior to admission)</div><div>ADLs: Totally independent (Barthel Index independent pre-admission)</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>[Family History:] 兒子</div><div><br></div><div>[Premorbid]</div><div>Living environment: 4F E+ with Son, wife</div><div>Occupation: 退休</div><div>Education: 大學？商管</div><div>Caregiver: 巴氏&nbsp;</div><div>Ambulation: community without device</div><div>ADLs: independent</div><div><br></div><div>[Consciousness &amp; Cognition]</div><div>Consciousness: E4M6V5, disoriented, follows simple 1-step order, 2-step inconsistently</div><div>Appearance: fair spirit</div><div>Response: cooperative</div><div>Mood: stable</div><div><br></div><div>[Mood Assessment]</div><div>PHQ-2:</div><div>1. Little interest: NA</div><div>2. Feeling down: NA</div><div><br></div><div>[4AT: 5]</div><div>1. Alertness: 0</div><div>2. AMT4 (Age, da